Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 14:01:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 50/53] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, a scanout buffer says it is a
 framebuffer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 3 (zero-copy scanout)
Why: the usage word this backend marshals into every NvGraphicBuffer
 carried HW_COMPOSER, HW_RENDER and HW_TEXTURE and not HW_FB. Those three
 describe a buffer the compositor consumes, the GPU draws into and a
 shader can sample. HW_FB is the one that says the display block itself
 reads it, which is precisely what a zero-copy swapchain image is for,
 and it was the only bit of the four left out.
 A gralloc usage word is a description, not a request, so an incomplete
 one is not rejected — it is believed. Nothing downstream can recover a
 bit the producer never claimed.
Evidence: GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_FB is 0x00001000 in libnx's
 switch/display/types.h, so the word this backend sends goes from 0xb00
 to 0x1b00. It reaches the queue through exactly two call sites, and
 they cannot disagree: nwindowConfigureBuffer records it from the first
 registered buffer into NWindow::usage, and wsi_horizon_try_dequeue
 passes that same NWindow::usage to every bqDequeueBuffer.
 NOT MEASURED ON HARDWARE. No console has run this. The claim being made
 is about which bit describes a scanout buffer, not about an observed
 change in behaviour, and if the queue were to refuse the bit it would
 do so at registration or at the first dequeue — at swapchain creation,
 loudly — rather than by handing back a buffer the display misreads.
Upstream: no — the whole file is Horizon-only.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MzkCx3cg9dXh1ncFb5ktHz
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 17 ++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 6a2f1155e3c..14f021926a5 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -83,9 +83,24 @@
  * compositor expects to find, not ours to choose. */
 #define WSI_HORIZON_GB_MAGIC 0xDAFFCAFFu
 #define WSI_HORIZON_GB_PID   42u
+/* GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_FB is the bit that says "this buffer is a
+ * framebuffer", i.e. one the display block itself reads. Every other bit
+ * describing what this buffer is for was here from the start —
+ * HW_COMPOSER because the compositor consumes it, HW_RENDER because the
+ * GPU draws into it, HW_TEXTURE because it can be sampled — and that one
+ * was not, which made the usage word describe a buffer for compositing
+ * rather than one for scanout.
+ *
+ * The same word goes to nwindowConfigureBuffer and, through
+ * NWindow::usage, to every bqDequeueBuffer, so producer and consumer
+ * cannot disagree about it. If the queue ever refused the bit, it would
+ * refuse it at registration or at the first dequeue — loudly, at
+ * swapchain creation — rather than by handing back a buffer the display
+ * reads wrongly. */
 #define WSI_HORIZON_GB_USAGE (GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_COMPOSER | \
                               GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_RENDER |   \
-                              GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_TEXTURE)
+                              GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_TEXTURE |  \
+                              GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_FB)
 
 /* The page-table entry kind an uncompressed colour surface gets on this
  * chip, and the only one the display block's "generic 16Bx2" sector
